title: '2026-11-15: taking back a reply is now one deletion, and forks nothing. Built and tested; the release build is yours.'
pid: 4e6c8fd8-461b-4517-b79c-0d26a17c1400
body: |2-
  Your mission of 08:20, 'Let a reader take back a reply to a message
  without being asked to fork the sender's note'. On a scratch server I
  reproduced Ed's finding as he wrote it: in a view of a foreign note
  switched to the user's perspective, 'delete' on the user's fork of a
  child asked to fork the note, with the reply under the fork or after
  deleting the reply first. The mend is in how the save reads a
  parent's content from the buffer, in Terry's files, not in the
  overrides: a deleted overrider now still stands for its original
  there, where that note is another's. Committed as 821b7907 on main.
  'bash/all-tests.sh --no-sound' passed, and the reader's suite by
  itself; the new server tests fail without the change. Two fresh
  reviewers read it: the first found that my first version, which
  applied the rule under the user's own notes too, changed what those
  saves did, so I narrowed it; the second found nothing serious, and I
  mended the docs it found loose. The reader's report did not change,
  so no reader test was added. A scratch server, after the narrowing,
  showed the take-back saving with no question and the message drawn
  again.
contains:
